module test.fixture.payload_arm_excess_field.carrier

import std.types { List, NonEmptyStr, String }

// THE SUBJECT OF THE EXCESS-FIELD ADMISSION MATRIX, DECLARED IN ITS OWN MODULE ON PURPOSE.
// test.claim.payload_arm_excess_field_admission compiles probe sources that IMPORT this module and
// construct the arm below, so the declaration and the record literal live in DIFFERENT modules --
// the same configuration a production construction site has. Declaring the type inside the probe
// source itself would have been one module, and a same-module literal is not the configuration the
// matrix means to characterize; the compiler reaches an imported arm through a different resolution
// path than a locally declared one, and a matrix that quietly tested the local path would report a
// rung for a path nobody uses.
//
// TWO ARMS, NOT ONE, AND THE SECOND IS NOT DECORATION. A single-arm coproduct is a type alias in
// this substrate, and an alias refuses at the IMPORTER rather than at the declaration, so a
// one-arm carrier would have made every probe fail to compile for a reason unrelated to the field
// under test -- the probes would have gone red on the carrier's shape and reported nothing about
// excess fields.
//
// PAYLOAD SHAPES MIRROR THE PRODUCTION CASE: one refinement-typed scalar and one collection, which
// is the shape whose excess-field judgment the matrix measures. The names are deliberately unlike
// anything in the production corpus because variant names resolve corpus-wide here, so a generic
// spelling in a fixture can collide with an unrelated module and turn a distant witness red.
type PayloadArmExcessFieldCarrier
  = PayloadArmProbeSubject { probe_carrier_host: NonEmptyStr, probe_carrier_members: List<String> }
  | PayloadArmProbeSibling { probe_carrier_note: String }
